---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Arms"
      eyebrow="There are none, and that is worth saying plainly"
      dek="No coat of arms belongs to this family. Several are sold as though one did. This page explains the difference, because it is the commonest way a family history goes wrong."
      >

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem;border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <p><strong>This family bore no arms, and this archive will not display any.</strong></p>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem">Search «Falco family crest» and you will be sold one within seconds.
    It will be handsome, it will very likely carry a falcon, and <strong>it will have nothing to do
    with the people in these registers.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the registers actually say they were</h2>
  <p>Every generation of this line that the registers describe is given a trade, and the trades are
  these: <strong><em>colono</em></strong> and <strong><em>contadino</em></strong> — men who worked
  another man's land — with <em>massaro</em> and <em>bracciale</em> appearing for the same men in
  different years. Generation six is entered as a <em>farmer</em>.</p>
  <p><strong>Arms in the Kingdom of Naples belonged to the propertied and the titled</strong>, and the
  registers are perfectly clear about who those were, because they write it down: the
  <em>possidenti</em>, the <em>proprietari</em>, the <em>gentiluomini</em>, the <em>Don</em> and the
  <em>Donna</em>. <strong>Arienzo had plenty of them.</strong> Don Giovanbattista Rivetti the
  apothecary; Don Francesco Morgillo, son of a doctor; the Cillo and the Troisi who between them
  supplied the town's mayors; Donna Angela Maria Lettieri, <em>proprietaria</em>.</p>
  <p><strong>None of them is this family.</strong> That is not a disappointment — it is the single
  most consistent fact the sweeps have produced, and it is why the same surnames keep appearing on
  the same nineteen streets for two hundred years.</p>

  <h2>The two things that look like heraldry and are not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The name itself.</strong> <em>Falco</em> is the Italian for falcon, so any arms
    invented for it will show one — <strong>canting arms, invented from the word, not inherited from a
    person.</strong> <a href={u("/name")}>The Name →</a></li>
    <li><strong>The «Don» in the registers.</strong> It appears constantly in these volumes and it is
    a courtesy title for the propertied and the clergy. <strong>It is never once applied to anyone in
    this line.</strong> Where the archive records a Don, it is recording a neighbour.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What would change this</h2>
  <p><strong>A grant, a seal, or a tomb.</strong> Not a website. If a document ever tied arms to a
  named ancestor in this descent, it would go on <a href={u("/documents")}>Documents</a> with its
  citation, like everything else. <strong>Until then this page stays as it is</strong> — because an
  archive that publishes its negatives has to publish this one too.</p>

  <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
    <a href={u("/name")}>Where the name comes from</a> ·
    <a href={u("/rule")}>Under whose rule they lived</a> ·
    <a href={u("/method")}>How this archive decides what to claim</a></p>
</Base>
